Mindy said it was genius that I was doing it so bad.

1458
00:56:16.160 --> 00:56:16.500
Yeah.

1459
00:56:16.740 --> 00:56:21.740
And I was texting with her because I was like, Mindy, do you remember this episode?

1460
00:56:21.740 --> 00:56:25.240
And she said, yes, I thought you were so funny that day.

1461
00:56:25.420 --> 00:56:26.640
You're Forrest Gump.

1462
00:56:26.820 --> 00:56:30.240
It was such a funny choice, you know, to be bad.

1463
00:56:30.780 --> 00:56:34.400
And I was like, Mindy, that was my literal best effort.

1464
00:56:35.640 --> 00:56:39.300
My acting limitations made me genius.

1465
00:56:39.520 --> 00:56:47.420
Like, that's one of those things where, like, if I tried to be bad, it wouldn't have been as good as me giving it my best effort.

1466
00:56:47.640 --> 00:56:49.160
I can't do it better than that.

1467
00:56:49.260 --> 00:56:50.100
I need to hear it.

1468
00:56:50.200 --> 00:56:51.080
I need to hear it.

1469
00:56:51.080 --> 00:56:52.000
I got to hear it.

1470
00:56:52.080 --> 00:56:52.940
I got to hear it.

1471
00:56:53.040 --> 00:56:53.880
I'm getting my laptop.

1472
00:56:53.980 --> 00:56:54.740
I got to hear it.

1473
00:56:55.040 --> 00:57:03.280
I got, now, with that information in my brain, I've got to hear it because I thought, like Mindy, that you were doing a bit.

1474
00:57:03.720 --> 00:57:04.160
No.

1475
00:57:04.500 --> 00:57:05.800
At, like, I'm going to read it.

1476
00:57:06.140 --> 00:57:08.040
My first gump, kind of crappy.

1477
00:57:08.420 --> 00:57:10.440
But no, it was your best effort, honey.

1478
00:57:10.600 --> 00:57:17.140
I listened to his voice in my head over and over and over and over again, and I tried to mimic it perfectly.

1479
00:57:17.140 --> 00:57:23.640
I have never loved you more, knowing now that that Forrest Gump was your earnest best effort.

1480
00:57:23.780 --> 00:57:24.220
It was.

1481
00:57:25.780 --> 00:57:28.620
Sales is like a box of chocolates.

1482
00:57:28.920 --> 00:57:32.140
You never know which vendor you're going to get.

1483
00:57:34.300 --> 00:57:35.200
Forrest Gump.

1484
00:57:35.980 --> 00:57:36.660
Lady.

1485
00:57:36.660 --> 00:57:39.340
That's the cutest thing ever.

1486
00:57:39.640 --> 00:57:41.440
That is the cutest thing.

1487
00:57:41.700 --> 00:57:42.260
Oh, my gosh.

1488
00:57:42.420 --> 00:57:44.020
Look at Devin and Cassie.

1489
00:57:44.160 --> 00:57:46.040
They're cracking up.

1490
00:57:46.360 --> 00:57:48.220
You guys, is that adorable?

1491
00:57:48.700 --> 00:57:49.360
Come on.

1492
00:57:49.920 --> 00:57:50.640
So cute.

1493
00:57:50.800 --> 00:57:51.420
We love it.

1494
00:57:52.140 --> 00:57:52.880
Thank you, guys.

1495
00:57:53.240 --> 00:57:53.920
Thank you.

1496
00:57:53.920 --> 00:57:56.040
I love doing that scene.

1497
00:57:56.260 --> 00:57:57.800
I loved the chainsaw.

1498
00:57:58.080 --> 00:57:59.140
I loved the chainsaw.

1499
00:57:59.140 --> 00:58:07.760
It was one of my most, you know, this was one of the few times when I was called upon to do, like, what would you call it?

1500
00:58:07.940 --> 00:58:09.160
Like, lead the group?

1501
00:58:09.360 --> 00:58:11.060
To be the comedy protagoniSt.

1502
00:58:11.480 --> 00:58:11.660
Yes.

1503
00:58:11.660 --> 00:58:13.820
I was usually the receiver of comedy.

1504
00:58:13.980 --> 00:58:19.480
My job on this show was to amplify a comedic moment with my deadpan reaction.

1505
00:58:19.480 --> 00:58:24.940
But in this case, I got to pitch the comedy ball, and it felt really good.

1506
00:58:25.140 --> 00:58:27.280
Other people reacted off of you.

1507
00:58:27.480 --> 00:58:27.840
Yes.

1508
00:58:28.060 --> 00:58:29.060
And it was fun.

1509
00:58:29.300 --> 00:58:39.480
Well, one of my favorite things to watch play out in any movie or TV show is when a character decides to step up to the plate and then bails.

1510
00:58:40.960 --> 00:58:47.520
So, at the end, when Pam got out the chainsaw and was like, cutting down the competition.

1511
00:58:48.040 --> 00:58:48.240
Yeah.

1512
00:58:48.240 --> 00:58:49.740
The bail is my favorite.

1513
00:58:49.900 --> 00:58:50.960
She's like, I'm out.

1514
00:58:51.500 --> 00:58:54.320
Well, during all this, Michael has gone over to Holly's desk.

1515
00:58:55.420 --> 00:58:57.980
He's stroking her yellow cardigan.

1516
00:58:58.160 --> 00:59:00.820
He cuts half of the sleeve off.

1517
00:59:01.040 --> 00:59:01.380
Yeah.

1518
00:59:01.900 --> 00:59:03.800
That made me laugh so hard, Jenna.

1519
00:59:04.440 --> 00:59:13.920
And then he, like, pushes the chair in, and this screensaver pops up of Martin Short, which is so perfect.

1520
00:59:14.100 --> 00:59:15.140
As Ed Grimley.

1521
00:59:15.400 --> 00:59:15.760
Yes.

1522
00:59:15.760 --> 00:59:18.280
You know what Randy Cordray told me?

1523
00:59:18.420 --> 00:59:18.680
What?

1524
00:59:19.120 --> 00:59:20.240
That little moment.

1525
00:59:20.700 --> 00:59:21.940
Like, that was a whole thing.

1526
00:59:22.120 --> 00:59:24.860
He said we had this graphic artist on staff.

1527
00:59:24.860 --> 00:59:26.140
His name was Michael Marcus.

1528
00:59:26.140 --> 00:59:35.240
And he had to build that on-screen computer graphic where there were these documents on the homepage, that picture of Martin Short.

1529
00:59:35.460 --> 00:59:40.020
They had to get permission to license that photo from Getty Images.

1530
00:59:40.700 --> 00:59:45.320
I mean, that little moment, that was someone's day creating that.

1531
00:59:45.500 --> 00:59:45.550
Right.

1532
00:59:45.550 --> 00:59:48.620
And I just, I love watching the show and thinking of those details.

1533
00:59:49.300 --> 00:59:54.200
Well, to finish out the Nashua Branch storyline, Pam and Michael, they pack up.

1534
00:59:54.320 --> 00:59:55.660
I love when Pam was like, don't look up.

1535
00:59:55.780 --> 00:59:56.360
Don't look up.

1536
00:59:56.480 --> 00:59:58.220
Don't look up when they're packing up the car.

1537
00:59:58.780 --> 01:00:00.100
And they go to a diner.

1538
01:00:00.100 --> 01:00:09.500
And Michael confesses that, A, he cut off part of her sweater and that he saw a Dear Michael folder on her desktop.

1539
01:00:09.500 --> 01:00:10.640
And he copied it.

1540
01:00:10.820 --> 01:00:11.720
He's got it.

1541
01:00:12.240 --> 01:00:14.440
And Pam says, you can't read that.

1542
01:00:14.960 --> 01:00:16.180
But I can read it.

1543
01:00:16.860 --> 01:00:17.260
Right.

1544
01:00:17.340 --> 01:00:18.060
Pam can read it.

1545
01:00:18.120 --> 01:00:22.600
And I won't tell you what the specifics are, but I can tell you if she still likes you.

1546
01:00:22.840 --> 01:00:24.860
We had a lot of fan mail about that.

1547
01:00:25.060 --> 01:00:27.440
Emma S., Eric M., and Zachary C.

1548
01:00:27.640 --> 01:00:30.180
all wanted to know, was there a letter?

1549
01:00:30.580 --> 01:00:33.160
Or was I just pretending to read something?

1550
01:00:33.840 --> 01:00:34.860
There was a letter.

1551
01:00:35.260 --> 01:00:36.640
I wondered that, too.

1552
01:00:36.840 --> 01:00:39.060
Phil Shea wrote a letter, and I was very grateful.

1553
01:00:40.000 --> 01:00:44.580
Because as I read it, it helped me get in a headspace.

1554
01:00:44.880 --> 01:00:52.420
Because there were all these things I read, but then I knew I was going to tell Michael the scripted lines of, like, it's not over.

1555
01:00:53.320 --> 01:00:54.200
And it was great.

1556
01:00:54.500 --> 01:00:58.620
It was so helpful as an actor to have not just a blank screen there.

1557
01:00:58.760 --> 01:01:00.680
So thank you, Phil Shea, for writing me a letter.

1558
01:01:00.880 --> 01:01:05.200
I wonder if Phil had to consult the writing staff and say, guys, I'm going to write this letter.

1559
01:01:05.360 --> 01:01:05.800
Do you have any?

1560
01:01:05.940 --> 01:01:07.420
Or he just did that on his own.

1561
01:01:07.420 --> 01:01:09.540
I think that was stuff he just did on his own.

1562
01:01:09.660 --> 01:01:13.040
He was a very detail-oriented prop master.

1563
01:01:13.260 --> 01:01:13.540
Yes.

1564
01:01:13.680 --> 01:01:17.040
And he would often do that if we had a fakie piece of paper.

1565
01:01:17.260 --> 01:01:17.780
A memo.

1566
01:01:18.300 --> 01:01:19.500
It was very detailed.

1567
01:01:19.780 --> 01:01:20.100
Mm-hmm.

1568
01:01:20.480 --> 01:01:21.760
I have a question for you.

1569
01:01:21.820 --> 01:01:25.500
Because when I watch this, I am always, like, relating it back to, like, things happening.

1570
01:01:25.980 --> 01:01:27.120
Would you have read it?

1571
01:01:27.480 --> 01:01:32.140
If you saw, like, something on your ex's computer, would you have read it?

1572
01:01:32.400 --> 01:01:32.800
Yeah.

1573
01:01:33.200 --> 01:01:33.920
Me, too.

1574
01:01:34.140 --> 01:01:34.620
Of course.

1575
01:01:35.000 --> 01:01:35.400
100%.

1576
01:01:35.400 --> 01:01:36.200
Yeah.

1577
01:01:36.360 --> 01:01:38.320
I would have just sat down right then and there and read it.

1578
01:01:38.540 --> 01:01:38.660
Correct.

1579
01:01:39.180 --> 01:01:39.460
Okay.

1580
01:01:39.460 --> 01:01:40.640
I feel better now.

1581
01:01:41.000 --> 01:01:41.180
Yeah.

1582
01:01:41.880 --> 01:01:46.800
Well, listen, Michael is thrilled to find out that it's not over with Holly.

1583
01:01:48.020 --> 01:01:53.560
And we're not sure yet what he's going to do with that information, but he's happy to know it.

1584
01:01:53.760 --> 01:01:55.040
He feels like he has closure.

1585
01:01:55.400 --> 01:01:55.620
Yeah.

1586
01:01:55.860 --> 01:01:56.560
He does.

1587
01:01:56.560 --> 01:02:01.740
We haven't talked about the rest of this birthday storyline.

1588
01:02:02.340 --> 01:02:05.140
Well, listen, they're not doing very well.

1589
01:02:05.360 --> 01:02:09.240
Jim and Dwight are still trying to brainstorm the perfect party for Kelly.

1590
01:02:09.560 --> 01:02:09.740
Yeah.

1591
01:02:10.260 --> 01:02:11.320
They're in the conference room.

1592
01:02:11.620 --> 01:02:24.560
Jim says, okay, so far, our ideal party consists of beer, fights to the death, cupcakes, blood pudding, blood, touch football, mating, charades, and, yes, horse hunting.

1593
01:02:25.220 --> 01:02:25.660
Yeah.

1594
01:02:26.100 --> 01:02:26.880
And Dwight's like, you're right.

1595
01:02:27.000 --> 01:02:27.720
Forget horse hunting.

1596
01:02:27.720 --> 01:02:28.300
It's stupid.

1597
01:02:29.500 --> 01:02:31.220
Just only lose that one.

1598
01:02:31.380 --> 01:02:31.480
Yeah.

1599
01:02:31.480 --> 01:02:32.740
All the rest are viable.

1600
01:02:33.060 --> 01:02:33.240
Yeah.

1601
01:02:33.340 --> 01:02:34.640
All the rest sound great.

1602
01:02:34.900 --> 01:02:38.540
But ultimately, they actually find a theme.

1603
01:02:39.380 --> 01:02:39.700
Mm-hmm.

1604
01:02:40.160 --> 01:02:41.940
Everyone gathers in the conference room.

1605
01:02:42.140 --> 01:02:43.000
Kelly comes in.

1606
01:02:43.240 --> 01:02:44.040
There's a cake.

1607
01:02:44.200 --> 01:02:45.320
Her name is spelled wrong.

1608
01:02:45.480 --> 01:02:45.800
Mm-hmm.

1609
01:02:45.940 --> 01:02:46.880
And there's a chiclet.

1610
01:02:47.460 --> 01:02:48.760
You know what a chiclet is?

1611
01:02:48.900 --> 01:02:49.380
I do.

1612
01:02:49.500 --> 01:02:51.140
It's a piece of gum and it's a square.

1613
01:02:51.140 --> 01:02:52.780
It's a little square piece of gum.

1614
01:02:53.040 --> 01:02:54.160
I love a chiclet.

1615
01:02:54.180 --> 01:02:55.060
I love a chiclet, too.

1616
01:02:55.060 --> 01:02:58.080
I like it that it has like a candy coating before I chew the gum.

1617
01:02:58.180 --> 01:02:59.080
I like the crunch.

1618
01:02:59.360 --> 01:02:59.740
Me, too.

1619
01:03:00.420 --> 01:03:02.560
Kelly wants to know what's up with the chiclet.

1620
01:03:02.840 --> 01:03:04.960
Jim says, that's the best part.

1621
01:03:05.280 --> 01:03:07.380
The chiclet represents a pillow.

1622
01:03:07.640 --> 01:03:09.540
It's a pillow or a television.

1623
01:03:10.060 --> 01:03:10.980
That's our theme.

1624
01:03:11.320 --> 01:03:14.020
A pillow or a television is their theme.

1625
01:03:14.260 --> 01:03:17.380
She gets to decide one hour of TV or an hour of napping.

1626
01:03:17.380 --> 01:03:18.620
What would you pick?

1627
01:03:18.980 --> 01:03:22.080
I would, oh, I don't know if I could sleep at work.

1628
01:03:22.320 --> 01:03:24.460
I can't nap in general.

1629
01:03:24.460 --> 01:03:27.540
I have to be sick in order to take a nap.

1630
01:03:27.700 --> 01:03:32.660
Even if I've gotten three hours of sleep the night before, I can't nap.

1631
01:03:32.900 --> 01:03:33.180
Same.

1632
01:03:33.180 --> 01:03:34.980
I get like wired tired.

1633
01:03:35.360 --> 01:03:40.160
So, I mean, I'm in the middle of the White Lotus.

1634
01:03:40.240 --> 01:03:42.080
I'm in the middle of Hacks.

1635
01:03:42.420 --> 01:03:45.080
I only just finished Mare of Easttown.

1636
01:03:45.220 --> 01:03:46.720
Do you hear how behind I am?

1637
01:03:47.420 --> 01:03:50.580
So, hour of TV, yes, please.

1638
01:03:50.840 --> 01:03:52.040
That sounds decadent to me.

1639
01:03:52.500 --> 01:03:54.740
Also, yeah, I can't sleep during the day.

1640
01:03:54.900 --> 01:03:57.440
Something has to be wrong with me if I fall asleep during the day.

1641
01:03:57.560 --> 01:03:57.720
Yeah.

1642
01:03:57.720 --> 01:03:59.200
It's just like my body just won't do it.

1643
01:03:59.700 --> 01:04:03.300
I do think this is a little bit of an old tech alert.

1644
01:04:03.580 --> 01:04:04.660
What's the old tech?

1645
01:04:04.960 --> 01:04:09.560
They say that the chiclet represents a pillow or a television.

1646
01:04:10.080 --> 01:04:10.460
Mm-hmm.

1647
01:04:10.820 --> 01:04:15.260
And I think at work now, we would just watch an hour on our phone or our computer.

1648
01:04:15.480 --> 01:04:16.100
That's true.

1649
01:04:16.100 --> 01:04:19.740
And also, doesn't Stanley say there's nothing good on right now?

1650
01:04:20.080 --> 01:04:22.720
Well, Creed says Bonnie Hunt's on.

1651
01:04:22.900 --> 01:04:25.780
But it's that they're going to watch like live television.

1652
01:04:25.920 --> 01:04:26.440
That's right.

1653
01:04:26.600 --> 01:04:27.880
Not streaming anything.

1654
01:04:27.880 --> 01:04:28.760
No streaming.

1655
01:04:29.060 --> 01:04:29.380
Mm-mm.

1656
01:04:29.700 --> 01:04:33.200
So, I would say their theme was a little bit of an old tech alert.

1657
01:04:33.440 --> 01:04:33.940
I agree.

1658
01:04:34.380 --> 01:04:36.300
Well, Kelly chooses nap.

1659
01:04:36.420 --> 01:04:39.240
And she takes a little nap under the conference table.

1660
01:04:39.460 --> 01:04:45.220
And while she does, Jim and Dwight sit on that little couch by reception.

1661
01:04:45.220 --> 01:04:46.460
And they eat their cake.

1662
01:04:46.460 --> 01:04:47.980
And they're very proud of themselves.

1663
01:04:48.280 --> 01:04:50.800
And I want to point out this scene to everyone.

1664
01:04:51.320 --> 01:04:58.880
Because John is doing a classic fakey, eaty acting technique with this cake.

1665
01:04:59.120 --> 01:05:00.840
He's shoving the food around the plate.

1666
01:05:00.840 --> 01:05:03.960
He would take a big scoop of cake.

1667
01:05:04.240 --> 01:05:07.640
But then, he smushes it back down.

1668
01:05:07.880 --> 01:05:12.200
And so, by the time he puts it in his mouth, it just has a little frosting on it.

1669
01:05:12.320 --> 01:05:12.600
Mm-hmm.

1670
01:05:12.820 --> 01:05:13.460
Watch it.

1671
01:05:13.620 --> 01:05:17.900
He goes for the cake three times and never actually eats a piece.

1672
01:05:18.200 --> 01:05:20.540
At the beginning, he eats like half a piece.

1673
01:05:21.140 --> 01:05:24.560
You have to do this because otherwise you're going to get sick.

1674
01:05:25.280 --> 01:05:30.060
However, sitting next to him is Rainn Wilson, who eats the cake.

1675
01:05:30.420 --> 01:05:30.940
Rainn?

1676
01:05:30.940 --> 01:05:32.700
Because Rainn always ate in scenes.

1677
01:05:32.900 --> 01:05:33.560
It didn't matter.

1678
01:05:33.820 --> 01:05:35.940
Remember, he ate so many bananas, he got sick.

1679
01:05:36.140 --> 01:05:36.340
Yeah.

1680
01:05:36.780 --> 01:05:39.580
When Rainn, as Dwight, does anything, he goes all out.

1681
01:05:39.760 --> 01:05:39.920
Yep.

1682
01:05:40.220 --> 01:05:44.580
And I feel like Rainn eats in scenes the way Brad Pitt eats in movies.

1683
01:05:44.980 --> 01:05:46.460
Watch in Ocean's 12.

1684
01:05:46.720 --> 01:05:47.940
The guy is always eating.

1685
01:05:48.160 --> 01:05:49.880
I wanted to know, like, is this a choice?

1686
01:05:49.880 --> 01:05:51.580
You're constantly eating something.

1687
01:05:51.780 --> 01:05:52.880
Watch Goodfellas.

1688
01:05:53.000 --> 01:05:55.460
Do you see how much steak and pasta those guys eat?

1689
01:05:55.620 --> 01:05:56.680
They dig in.

1690
01:05:56.900 --> 01:05:58.100
Goodfellas makes me hungry.

1691
01:05:58.340 --> 01:05:58.700
Yeah.

1692
01:05:58.820 --> 01:06:00.060
When I watch it, I get hungry.

1693
01:06:00.220 --> 01:06:00.640
For sure.

1694
01:06:01.440 --> 01:06:03.200
Guys, guess what?

1695
01:06:03.460 --> 01:06:05.460
That was Lecture Circuit Part 2.

1696
01:06:05.800 --> 01:06:06.200
Mm-hmm.

1697
01:06:06.520 --> 01:06:08.500
With a little bit of movie.

1698
01:06:08.620 --> 01:06:09.240
Extra stuff.

1699
01:06:09.360 --> 01:06:10.140
Extra stuff.

1700
